// Title: PsExec Service Child Process Execution as LOCAL SYSTEM
// Sigma ID: 7c0dcd3d-acf8-4f71-9570-f448b0034f94
// Status: test | Level: high
// Author: Florian Roth (Nextron Systems)
// Date: 2022-07-21 | Modified: 2023-02-28
// ATT&CK: execution
// Description: Detects suspicious launch of the PSEXESVC service on this system and a sub process run as
//   LOCAL_SYSTEM (-s), which means that someone remotely started a command on this system running it
//   with highest privileges and not only the privileges of the login user account (e.g. the
//   administrator account)
// False positives:
//   - Users that debug Microsoft Intune issues using the commands mentioned in the official documentation; see https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/mem/intune/apps/intune-management-extension
// References:
//   - https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sysinternals/downloads/psexec
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/05d1031f537e830b76da53bae2400c5814c89165/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_sysinternals_psexesvc_as_system.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, live Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where InitiatingProcessFolderPath =~ "C:\\Windows\\PSEXESVC.exe" and (AccountName contains "AUTHORI" or AccountName contains "AUTORI")
